So, for the given complex number $\sqrt{2} e^{11 \pi i/4}$, we have $\theta = \frac{11\pi}{4}$. Note that $\frac{11\pi}{4}$ is equivalent to $\frac{3\pi}{4}$ because $\frac{11\pi}{4} = 3\pi - \frac{\pi}{4} = 2\pi + \frac{3\pi}{4}$, and the complex number $e^{(2\pi + \theta)i} = e^{\theta i}$ because $e^{2\pi i} = 1$. 

Therefore, $e^{11 \pi i/4} = e^{3\pi i/4} = \cos\left(\frac{3\pi}{4}\right) + i\sin\left(\frac{3\pi}{4}\right)$. 

The rectangular form of $\cos\left(\frac{3\pi}{4}\right) + i\sin\left(\frac{3\pi}{4}\right)$ is $-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} + i\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$. 

So, $\sqrt{2} e^{11 \pi i/4} = \sqrt{2} \left(-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} + i\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right) = -1 + i$. 

The rectangular form of $\sqrt{2} e^{11 \pi i/4}$ is \boxed{-1 + i}.
